%! TEX root = ../main.tex

\chapter{Determination of an Absolute Energy Scale}
\label{chap:xy-scanner}

The analysis in the subsequent chapters heavily relies on information measured 
by the \WCD, \SSD, and \UMD. With the inauguration of the latter two detectors 
ocurring roughly ten years after the last official measurement of \FD pixel 
calibration constants \cite{dorofeevFDAbsoluteCalibration2013}, the 
cross-calibration of the reconstructed \UMD/\SSD energies (Ref.\ 
\cref{sssec:sd-sd-reconstruction-energy-estimator}) depend on measurement data 
that are more than a decade old. 

While the pixel calibration constants are in principle updated using a nightly 
relative calibration (Ref.~\cref{sssec:relative-calibration}) it is crucial to 
verify the integrity of the relative tracking and the employed energy 
calibration.

The XY-Scanner is a novel end-to-end calibration system that is used to 
estimate the \FD pixel calibration constants with an accuracy of 
\todo{uncertainty}. It provides an excellent method of verifying the validity 
of the currently employed pixel calibration constants, and the \FD energy scale
by extension. The measurement system as well as underlying analysis is 
presented in detail in \cite{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}, and 
summarized in \cref{sec:xy-scanner}.

\cref{sec:quality-assurance} details extensive quality checks that have been
developed in this work, and that are used to assure the reproducibility of XY 
measurements and establish the trustworthiness of the gathered data.

After ensuring the quality of the novel calibration method, the results of 
various XY measurement campaigns are compared to current pixel calibration 
constants can. \cref{sec:telescope-aging} details this comparison and details
possible reasons for discrepancies. An updated relative calibration tracking 
algorithm is equally given.

Finally, an update to the \FD energy scale is presented in 
\cref{sec:fd-energy-scale}.

\section{The XY-Scanner}
\label{sec:xy-scanner}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{xy-scanner/xy_light_source.jpg}
  \label{fig:xy-light-source}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{xy-scanner/xy_pattern.png}
  \label{fig:xy-flash-pattern}
  }\hfill
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/xy_trace_example.png}
  \label{fig:xy-example-trace}}
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:xy-light-source} The XY calibration light source 
  mounted in a test setup in Karlsruhe. Photo from 
  \cite{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023} \subref{fig:xy-flash-pattern} The
  XY-Scanner, consisting of light source, horizontal and vertical axles, as 
  well as the electronics box on the top right, mounted in front of an \FD 
  telescope. Photo from \cite{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}. Flasher 
  positions are superimposed as grey hexagons. \subref{fig:xy-example-trace} 
  Response of a single pixel to the flash of the XY-Scanner. Signal start and 
  end are marked.}
  \label{fig:xy-hardware}
\end{figure}

There exist a plethora of calibration methods for the \PAO \FD telescopes
(Ref.~\cref{ssec:fd-calibration}), though they all build upon the same 
principle; Exposing the \FD-cameras to a known amount of light, typically in
the form of short light pulses, allows one to tie the signal recorded in the 
pixel-\PMTs to an incident count of photons, which, during normal \DAQ can be 
used to estimate the energy of an \EAS
(Ref.~\cref{sssec:fd-reconstruction-energy-estimator}).

The \CLS in the case of the XY-Scanner calibration is a handball sized 
integrating sphere (see \cref{fig:xy-light-source}), mounted on namesake XY 
translation stages (\cref{fig:xy-flash-pattern}) that move the \CLS to 
positions in a hexagonal coordinate system spanning the entire aperture. At 
each point the \CLS emits a flash that is recorded by the \FD. An example
response to a single flash is shown in \cref{fig:xy-example-trace}.

\subsection{Light Source Calibration}
\label{ssec:xy-absolute-calibration}

The precise determination of $C^{i}_\mathrm{abs}$ requires an accurate 
measurement of the \CLS radiance $L_\mathrm{CLS}$. That is to say that the 
calibration light source itself must be calibrated.

Different facilities have been set up to measure the radiance of the XY \LED 
independently \cite{heibgesPhotometricCharacterizationLight2020, 
schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023, vaculaVyvojKalibracnihoSvetelneho2024}. 
While precise implementation details differ, all test banks rely on measuring a
distance-dependent flux, $\Phi_\mathrm{PD}(r)$, of photons with wavelength 
$\lambda$ on a \PD illuminated by the \CLS. This flux follows, to first order,
an inverse-square law, supplemented by higher order corrections that consider 
the view factor $F_{\mathrm{PD},\,\mathrm{CLS}}$ that depends on the aperture 
radii $R_\mathrm{PD}$, $R_\mathrm{CLS}$ of the \PD- and \CLS aperture 
respectively.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:source-radiance}
F_{\mathrm{PD},\,\mathrm{CLS}} = \frac{1}{2}\,\left[ 1 + \left(\frac{r-r_0}{R_\mathrm{CLS}}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{R_\mathrm{PD}}{R_\mathrm{CLS}}\right)^2 - \sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{r-r_0}{R_\mathrm{CLS}}\right)^2 - 3\left(\frac{R_\mathrm{PD}}{R_\mathrm{CLS}}\right)^2} \right] \\
\end{equation}

The diode at distance $r-r_0$ from the \CLS consequently measures a flux 

\begin{equation}
\Phi_\mathrm{PD}(r) = \frac{hc}{\lambda}\,\tilde{S}_\mathrm{setup}\,L_\mathrm{CLS},
\end{equation}

where $\tilde{S}_\mathrm{setup}$ in \cref{eq:source-radiance} gives the 
response function of the \PD.\footnote{Which will in general depend on the 
entire test setup, not just the \PD.} By controlling the laboratory environment
precisely, the source radiance can be determined with an accuracy of 
$\lessapprox\SI{3}{\percent}$. \cref{fig:abs-calib-measurements} and 
\todo{abscalpaper} detail the agreement of the cross-calibration between 
different measurement facilities.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/abs_calib_jlo.png}
  \label{fig:abs-calib-jlo}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/abs_calib_kit.png}
  \label{fig:abs-calib-kit}
  }
  \caption[]{The signal response versus distance of a \PD is used to estimate 
  the source radiance of the \CLS. Shown are results for the same \CLS tested 
  \subref{fig:abs-calib-jlo} in Olomouc, Czech Republic, and 
  \subref{fig:abs-calib-kit} in Karlsruhe, Germany.}
  \label{fig:abs-calib-measurements}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Telescope Calibration}
\label{ssec:fd-xy-calibration}

After the calibration of the \CLS the \FD pixel-\PMTs can be calibrated. For 
this purpose the XY-Scanner iteratively illuminates the entire aperture in 
$n_\mathrm{pos} > 1500$ unique flashes at different positions. The telescope 
response is recorded for each flash. The measurement process takes on average
$\mathcal{O}(\SI{1}{\hour})$, including setup, and auxiliary Calibration A 
measurements that check camera stability. The order of operation sees two 
Calibration A measurements before and after each XY-Scan.

\begin{equation*}
	\mathrm{Cal\,A}\,\rightarrow\,\mathrm{Cal\,A\,(open\,shutter})\,
	\rightarrow\,\mathrm{XY}\,\rightarrow\,\mathrm{Cal\,A\,(open\,shutter)}
	\,\rightarrow\,\mathrm{Cal\,A}.
\end{equation*}

The gathered measurement information from each flash of the XY-Scan may then be 
analyzed to calculate $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ via the following steps:

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{enumerate}
	\item The baseline $b_i$ is calculated per \PMT and subtracted from all
	$n_\mathrm{pos}$ traces $t_i$.
	\item A pulse finding algorithm 
	(Ref.~\cite[][Section 7.1.1]{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}) 
	determines the trace interval $[k_\mathrm{start}, k_\mathrm{stop}]$ 
	that contains the light pulse from the calibration light source.
\item The pulse integral (named signal in the following) is calculated per 
	trace. The sum of all $n_\mathrm{pos}$ integrals yield a total signal 
	per pixel in units of \ADC.
	\item The total number of photons received by each pixel-\PMT is 
	estimated via the known source radiance. The non-uniform camera 
	geometry and reflectivity are taken into account via simulation based 
	correction factors $F_{i,\,\mathrm{CLS}}$.
	\item The total signal in every \PMT is divided by the total count of 
	incident photons to calculate the pixel calibration constant in units 
	of $\ADC/\gamma$.
\end{enumerate}
\vspace{0.3cm}

In summary, the calibration constant $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ for pixel $i$ is 
calculated as 

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:xy-scanner-calib-calculation}
C^i_\mathrm{abs} = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{n_\mathrm{pos}} \left[ \sum\limits_{k = k_\mathrm{start}}^{k_\mathrm{stop}} t_{i,\,k} - b_i \right]\,\bigg/\,n_\mathrm{pos}L_\mathrm{CLS}\,F^i_\mathrm{CLS}.
\end{equation}

The different systematic and statistical contributions intrinsic to this 
process are listed in \cref{tab:xy-systematics}, where a comparison to the drum
(Ref.~\cref{sssec:drum-calib}) is also offered. The total uncertainty on 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ estimated by the XY-Scanner amounts to \todo{uncertainty}, 
which represents an improvement over the drum calibration by \todo{} percentage 
points.

