\section{Quality Assurance}
\label{sec:quality-assurance}

By calculating \FD pixel calibration constants as a sum over many individual 
light flashes according to \cref{eq:xy-scanner-calib-calculation}, several
implicit assumptions are made, namely

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begingroup
\renewcommand\arraystretch{1.3}
\begin{itemize}
\item that the source radiance $L_\mathrm{CLS}$ remains constant during the 
measurement.
\item The \CLS flashes cover the complete filter, and the aperture is 
unoccluded.
\item The sensitivity of the \FD pixel-\PMTs is constant during the 
measurement.
\end{itemize}
\endgroup
\vspace{0.2cm}

Thus, in order to ensure that the XY-Scanner delivers accurate and precise 
pixel calibration constants we must verify that these assumptions hold. This 
can be done most easily by establishing a checklist of tests that are executed 
and that must to pass before accepting the results of XY measurement runs.

The following sections detail what these tests are, and how failure states are 
defined based on outlier identification and cross-correlation of available XY
data.

\subsection{Preselection of XY Runs}
\label{ssec:xy-qa-run-selection}

To date, six measurement campaigns have been conducted that have probed the
calibration constants in all 27 telescopes at least once. With the XY-Scanner 
actively being developed during the early phases of measuring, many different 
run configurations have been tested throughout the different campaigns. 

A long-term strategy has emerged from the prototyping phase that compromises 
between ease-of-use, long-term feasibility, and systematic uncertainty 
\cite{filipQualityAssuranceXY2024}. The run configuration of the XY-Scanner 
measurements are the following:\footnote{Details on the nomenclature are found in 
\cite{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}.}

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{itemize}
\item Use a type \textbf{3P-GPS-053-S} integrating sphere sporting a modified 
baffle to improve the Lambertian diffuse emission profile of the XY light 
source.

\item Install \textbf{Generation 2 LED head} in the integrating sphere, which 
supports three \LEDs of type UVLED365-110E 
\cite{alldatasheet.comUVLED365110EDownload}. A thin diffusive material that 
covers the \LEDs, as well as a shielding baffle improve the emission profile.

\item Employ a \textbf{stepsize of 6 cm}. The step size between individual 
flasher positions directly influences the coverage factor of the telescope 
aperture. The smaller the step size, the lower the systematic uncertainty of the
calibration constants

\item Flash the \LED using a \textbf{current of 15.9 mA}, which compromises 
between \FD signal strength (and thus accuracy) and long-term operation of the 
\LED.

\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.4cm}

All data shown in the upcoming quality assurance analysis has been preselected
to adhere to these standards. A full list of run numbers can be found in
\cref{tab:xy-runlist}. 

\subsection{Light Source Stability}

The XY \LED is located inside the \CLS together with a \PD that logs the 
intensity of each \LED flash. The \PD is not calibrated precisely, but accurate
enough in order to monitor the nominal \LED operation. Moreover, temperature 
sensors are installed within the sphere to survey measurement conditions. Both 
sensors can be used to verify the stable operation of the \CLS \LED.



\subsubsection{Temperature}
\label{sssec:xy-qa-temperature}

The emission characteristics of the \CLS \LED are temperature dependent 
\cite{alldatasheet.comUVLED365110EDownload}. For this reason, the inside of the
integrating sphere is climate controlled and operates at a constant temperature
of $\SI{30}{\celsius}$. External factors such as wind gusts can cause temporary
deviations from the nominal. In an extreme case, this influences the total 
emitted signal, and pixel calibration constants as a consequence.

We estimate the influence of temperature deviations on the emissivity by 
examining both the camera signal,\footnote{Being the sum of all pixel signals 
per single flash.} as well as the (less precise) pulse response of the internal
\PD. Both are normalized to $L_\mathrm{CLS}$.

As the \PD is always at the same location w.r.t.~the XY \LED, it can be 
examined as is. For the \FD signal, to isolate effects of temperature 
fluctuations, only flashes at the exact center of the aperture are 
compared to one another. To account for varying \FD camera efficiency the 
integral signal is also normalized to the mean camera signal during the 
corresponding open shutter calibration A measurements.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/fd_signal_temperature_dependence.png}
  \label{fig:temperature-dependence-fd}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/pd_signal_temperature_dependence.png}
  \label{fig:temperature-dependence-pd}
  }
  \caption[]{ The influence of the \LED temperature on the flash intensity as seen by 
  \subref{fig:temperature-dependence-fd} the \FD-camera and \subref{fig:temperature-dependence-pd} 
  the less precise internal \PD. The red lines give linear fits to the mean signal per temperature 
  for temperatures with $>10$ recorded flashes.}
  \label{fig:temperature-stability}
\end{figure}

\cref{fig:temperature-stability} shows the distribution of recorded signals for
the \FD and the \PD. A linear fit of either detector observable yields a 
vanishing correlation between signal strength and \LED temperature. No 
significant trend is identifiable in both cases.

Expecting in theory a positive correlation between temperature and \LED 
emissivity in the considered temperature ranges 
(Ref.~\cite[p.~2, lower left]{alldatasheet.comUVLED365110EDownload}), we 
construct quality cuts by forming the very cautious assumption that flashes 
outside the temperature range from $\SIrange{29}{31}{\celsius}$ deviate from 
the mean signal by $\approx\SI{5}{\percent}$. 

With $n_\mathrm{pos} \gg 1000$ flashes per run, a single flash outside of this
2-degree range impacts the total signal by less than $1\permil$. Limiting the 
allowed magnitude of the temperature dependency on the emitted signal to 
$<\SI{0.2}{\percent}$ (Ref.~\cref{tab:xy-systematics}), we accept an XY run if 
it has $\leq4$ flashes where 
$T_\mathrm{LED}\notin[\SI{29}{\celsius},\,\SI{31}{\celsius}]$.

While the aforementioned assumption of a $\SI{5}{\percent}$ bias is very 
conservative, the effect on data is marginal. Most runs have all data points 
within $\SIrange{29}{31}{\celsius}$. There exists one run with a single flash at
$\SI{28.5}{\celsius}$, and two (rejected) runs with 11 and 19 bad temperatures 
respectively. Both are clear outliers in this analysis.

\subsubsection{Temporal Drifts}
\label{sssec:xy-qa-led-drift}

The pulse-to-pulse stability of the \CLS during radiance measurements is 
\SI{2}{\percent} \cite[p.~100]{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}. We can 
verify that this assumption holds during XY-Scans as well by analyzing the same
data as in the previous \cref{sssec:xy-qa-temperature}.

Focusing on the \PD signal normalized to the respective light source radiance, 
we identify possible drifts in the \CLS brightness during the scan by fitting
a first order polynomial to it for each run, assuming a systematic uncertainty 
of the form $\mathcal{N}(\upmu = \SI{0.583}{\ADC\per\giga\photon},\, 
\upsigma^2=\SI{0.009}{\square\ADC\per\giga\square\photon})$ 
(Ref.~\cref{fig:sigma-pd}).

With the resulting linear model $\hat{S}_\mathrm{PD}$ that approximates the 
measured signal $S_\mathrm{PD}$ for each flash, it becomes trivial to check the 
minimum pulse-to-pulse stability by calculating the difference in 
$\hat{S}_\mathrm{PD}$ between the first and last flash of the XY run.

\cref{fig:pd-drift} shows the average extrapolated pulse-to-pulse stability is 
well below \SI{1}{\percent}. The \CLS \LED operates stable during \DAQ. No runs 
are rejected. 

In practice, we are unable to distinguish a drift in the \LED from a drift in 
the \PD, meaning there exists the possibility to discard "good" data due to a 
"bad" \PD. This makes the above quality cut a cautious, but for the time being,
correct choice.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth]{xy-scanner/sigma_pd.png}
  \label{fig:sigma-pd}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth]{xy-scanner/pd_drift.png}
  \label{fig:pd-drift}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth]{xy-scanner/pd_interruptions.png}
  \label{fig:pd-interruptions}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:sigma-pd} Noise level of the \CLS \PD, normalized to
  the respective $L_\mathrm{CLS}$. \subref{fig:pd-drift} Difference in \PD 
  signal strength between the first and last flash extrapolated from a linear 
  fit. The red histogram marks runs that have already been rejected in 
  \cref{sssec:xy-qa-temperature}. \subref{fig:pd-interruptions} Difference in 
  mean \PD signal strength immediately before and after \DAQ interruptions.}
  \label{fig:pd-noise-level-drift-interruptions}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Interruptions}

If the XY-Scanner encounters hardware or software problems during a measurement
the run must be stopped and the problem is addressed manually. The reason for 
the interruption may lay in a communication timeout (especially frequent prior to
November 2023 \cite{filipFixfilespy2023}), or the XY-Scanner path being blocked.
We must estimate the influence of these \DAQ interruptions on the \CLS stability.

For the sake of this discussion, an interruption is classified as the scenario
in which more than $\SI{20}{\second}$ pass between consecutive \CLS 
flashes.\footnote{The average time between flashes is \SI{1.7}{\second}.}
Analyzing the relevant data and taking $\langle S^{<}_\mathrm{PD}\rangle$ 
($\langle S^{>}_\mathrm{PD}\rangle$) as the signal immediately before (after) 
the \DAQ interruption averaged over 20 flashes, we reject an XY run if it had an
interruption, and if $\left| \langle S^{<}_\mathrm{PD}\rangle - \langle 
S^{>}_\mathrm{PD}\rangle\right| \geq \SI{2}{\percent}$. The distribution of this
test statistic is shown in \cref{fig:pd-interruptions}. Interruptions, even over
a longer period ($>\SI{5}{\minute}$), do not seem to influence \DAQ, and all
runs pass this selection criterion 

\subsection{Measurement Setup}

The XY-Scanner is a temporary installment before the \FD aperture. An improper 
setup, e.g.~a misaligned \CLS is a possibility with every new measurement. In 
such a scenario the XY-Scanner will not (uniformly) illuminate the entire \FD
aperture. The correction factors and geometric calculations in 
\cref{eq:xy-scanner-calib-calculation} are not valid anymore and the 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ calculated from a misaligned XY run will be inaccurate.

\subsubsection{Positional alignment}
\label{sssec:xy-qa-filter-estimation}

We first check that the coordinate system of the XY-Scanner is correctly set,
meaning that the point $(0, 0)$ coincides with the center of the \FD aperture. 
Flasher positions are defined relative to this origin, implying that a 
misalignment between the two reference points will result in an improperly 
illuminated aperture.

Examining \cref{fig:filter-structure-real}, we note that the \UV filter of every
\FD telescope is segmented by a (around the center of the aperture) 
reflectionally symmetric support structure. This support structure blocks a 
measureable quantity of light during XY-Scans 
(Ref.~\cref{fig:filter-structure-estimated}), and can be used to estimate the 
position of the XY-Scanner relative to the \FD filter.

We identify the shadow of the filter structure by first grouping the flasher 
positions into rows and columns. We restrict our analysis to flasher positions 
where $\SI{500}{\milli\meter} < r < \SI{1100}{\milli\meter}$. This is the region
of the aperture where the \FD mirror is only occluded by the filter structure 
(and not by e.g.~the \FD-camera).

Next, the average signal $\langle S \rangle$ recorded by the \FD is calculated 
for every row and column. A first estimate of the filter arm positions follow 
from this by searching for peaks in the negative average signal, we require the 
peaks to have a prominence of $>\SI{7}{\percent}$ and a peak-to-peak distance of
$>\SI{480}{\milli\meter}$.\footnote{This corresponds to passing 
\lstinline[language=python]|\{prominence=0.07*max(-cols), distance=14\}| and
\lstinline[language=python]|\{prominence=0.07*max(-rows), distance=8\}| to 
pythons \lstinline[language=python]|scipy.signal.find_peaks|
\cite{virtanenSciPy10Fundamental2020}.} As evident in 
\cref{fig:filter-alignment}, the found locations coincide well with the true 
position of filter arms.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.50\textwidth]{xy-scanner/filter_structure.png}
  \label{fig:filter-structure-estimated}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.44\textwidth]{xy-scanner/filter_structure_real.png}
  \label{fig:filter-structure-real}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:filter-structure-estimated} Integral camera signal 
  recorded at every flash position. The two tiny subplots give the average \FD
  signal per column/row for flashes contained within the two red circles. 
  \subref{fig:filter-structure-real} Example image of the \FD filter structure.
  From \cite{horvathRequestImages2024}.}
  \label{fig:filter-alignment}
\end{figure}

Finally, because the filter structure can fall between a row/column, the exact
$x$ and $y$ coordinates are interpolated by calculating a weighted average over
the nearest neighbors of the peak row/column $p$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:filter-position}
X = \sum\limits_{i = p-1}^{p+1} \frac{\langle S \rangle_i}{\langle S \rangle_p} \,x_i\,\bigg/\,\sum\limits_{i = p-1}^{p+1} \frac{\langle S \rangle_i}{\langle S \rangle_p},\qquad
Y = \sum\limits_{i = p-1}^{p+1} \frac{\langle S \rangle_i}{\langle S \rangle_p} \,y_i\,\bigg/\,\sum\limits_{i = p-1}^{p+1} \frac{\langle S \rangle_i}{\langle S \rangle_p}
\end{equation}

The uncertainty on the filter position is at most of the order of the XY 
stepsize. In practice, the filter center, calculated as the mean position of all
found filter structures, is grouped much tighter and offers a sub-cm precision, 
as shown in \cref{fig:cls-alignment}. The XY-Scanner coordinate system is well
aligned with the \FD aperture. 

In order for an XY calibration to pass the positional alignment check, we 
require the calculated filter center to lie within \SI{20}{\milli\meter} of the 
XY origin. Only one run is rejected by this requirement. For further details on 
the rejected run see \cref{sssec:xy-qa-aperture-occlusion}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/CLS_alignment.png}
  \label{fig:cls-alignment}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/curtain_block.png}
  \label{fig:occluded-aperture}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:cls-alignment} Location of the filter center (blue) and
  signal barycenter (red) for all considered XY runs. 
  \subref{fig:occluded-aperture} Radial distribution of camera signal for all 
  XY runs. The white dashed line signifies the edge of the \FD aperture. Runs 
  with flashes within the red rectangle are rejected due to aperture occlusion.}
  \label{fig:measurement-setup-checks}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Directional alignment}

Next we ensure that the \CLS faces the \FD-camera as intended. That is to say
the plexiglass surface of the spheres exit port is parallel to the \UV-filter. 

Given the Lambertian emission characteristic of the \CLS, a misaligned light
source will overilluminate one portion of the mirror while underexposing
other parts. This effect will be visible in the comparison of camera signal 
corresponding to flashes at different positions of the aperture.

Thus, in order to check directional alignment of the \CLS, we calculate the
barycenter of all light flashes (weighing by the corresponding integral \FD 
signal) and require for its $x$ coordinate to be within \SI{60}{\milli\meter} of
the origin. We restrict this quality check to one dimension only because, due to
the camera blocking part of the mirror from view, we do not expect a symmetric 
distribution of signals in the $y$ direction. Moreover, given the measurement 
setup, it is easier to misalign the \CLS on the $x$-axis, calling for special 
care here.

\cref{fig:cls-alignment} shows the distribution of signal barycenters from all 
considered XY measurements. None of the examined runs fail this quality check.

\subsubsection{Aperture Occlusion}
\label{sssec:xy-qa-aperture-occlusion}

Looking more closely at the run rejected in 
\cref{sssec:xy-qa-filter-estimation}, it becomes apparent that the reason for 
rejection is the curtain being placed inside the camera \FOV, and the filter-
finding-algorithm misidentifying the shadow left by the curtain as an additional
filter structure. 

We can search for more instances of an abnormally occluded aperture by examining
outliers in the radial distribution of camera signals. For every XY run, we 
normalize the camera signal of each flash to the maximum camera signal recorded 
throughout the measurement, and bin them according to the radial position of the
XY-Scanner at the time of recording. \cref{fig:occluded-aperture} shows the 
histogram this data.

Outside of the camera shadow ($r>\SI{500}{\milli\meter}$), the signal recorded 
by the \FD reaches its maximum (and approximately constant) value, whereas 
almost no light is received at $r>\SI{1100}{\milli\meter}$, outside the \FD 
aperture.

Notably, there also exists a small fraction of flashes that are emitted within
full view of the camera where $<\SI{10}{\percent}$ of light ends up in the \FD.
If this occurs within the aforementioned radial distance 
$\SI{500}{\milli\meter}<r\leq\SI{1100}{\milli\meter}$ we reject the run.

Apart from the XY measurement with the curtain in view, an additional run is 
identified as problematic due to the obstruction of a single flash for which the
\FD-camera did not register any signal. Both are displayed in
\cref{fig:failed-runs}.

\subsection{Camera Stability}

The last remaining component in the XY-\DAQ chain that needs to be checked is the
\FD-camera. It is crucial that the camera operates in a stable fashion throughout 
the duration of the XY scan, this can be ensured via the following selection cuts.

\subsubsection{Camera Sensitivity}
\label{sssec:xy-qa-camera-sensitivity}

The (assumed constant) sensitivity of the \FD-camera is checked by comparing the
open shutter Calibration A measurements before and after the XY scan. If the 
camera response to both exposures is the same, it is reasonable to assume that 
the sensitivity of the pixel-\PMTs remains constant throughout the XY scan. 

In the case of a nonzero drift, the total uncertainty on the pixel signal will 
depend on the magnitude of a drift in the \FD-camera sensitivity, to first order
expressed as 
$\langle S^\mathrm{CalA}_\mathrm{max}\,/\,S^\mathrm{CalA}_\mathrm{min}\rangle$, 
where $S^\mathrm{CalA}_\mathrm{max}$ ($S^\mathrm{CalA}_\mathrm{min}$) give the
pixel-wise maximum (minimum) between the pre- and post-XY Calibration A 
measurements. 

Assuming further that the drift in \FD-camera sensitivity is approximately 
linear over the on average $\SI{40}{\minute}$ \DAQ period, the signal 
uncertainty in the $i$th pixel signal is at most 
$\upsigma_{\mathrm{max},\,i}=(S^\mathrm{CalA}_{\mathrm{max},\,i}\, / 
\,S^\mathrm{CalA}_{\mathrm{min},\,i} - 1)\,/\,2$, implying that on average

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:total-error-cal-a}
\langle \upsigma_\mathrm{max} \rangle = \sum\limits_{i=1}^{n_\mathrm{pixel}} \frac{\upsigma_{\mathrm{max},\,i}}{440} 
\end{equation}

over the entire camera. Capping $\langle\upsigma_\mathrm{max}\rangle$ to a maximum
of \SI{0.2}{\percent}, we identify two runs that have a large drift in camera 
sensitivity (Ref.~\cref{fig:cal-a-errors}). The corresponding ratios of the 
Calibration A signal are shown in \cref{fig:cal-a-ratio}, clearly, the rejected 
runs do not follow the underlaying distribution visible in all runs.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/cal_a_stability.png}
  \label{fig:cal-a-ratio}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/cal_a_total_error.png}
  \label{fig:cal-a-errors}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:cal-a-ratio} Ratio of Calibration A pixel signals for 
  all tested runs. The ratio distribution of rejected runs (red) differs notably
  from the underlying (black) distribution and dominates its tail. 
  \subref{fig:cal-a-errors} Distribution of the average error in the camera 
	signal extrapolated from a linear drift in the Calibration A signal.}
  \label{fig:cal-a-stability}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Catch All Sanity Check}

Finally, we impose a restriction on the \FD-camera signal per flash. This 
restriction is not (strictly) based on physics, but rather meant as a catch-all 
sanity check meant to identify anything from trigger issues (e.g.~Run 
$\#05616\mathrm{x}$ in Oct.~2022 at \HEAT1) to failures in the \DAQ readout 
system (e.g.~Run $\#15672$ in Oct.~2022 at \LL4).

The number of photons emitted by the \CLS per flash is of the order 
$\mathcal{O}(10^9)$ and constant throughout the XY-Scan. With calibration 
constants below $\SI{10}{\upgamma\per\ADC}$ the camera response should be of 
comparable magnitude, albeit dependant, of course, on the \CLS position in front
of the aperture.

Binning the camera response over all considered runs, we see an approximately
Gaussian distribution with variance $\upsigma^2 = \SI{53.73}{\mega\ADC\squared}$ 
(Ref.~\cref{fig:fd-event-sum}). We reject a run if any flash has an associated 
camera signal $|S-\langle S\rangle| > \SI{100}{\mega\ADC}$, corresponding to a 
significance of $|S-\langle S\rangle|\,/\,\upsigma = 13.64$. The probability of 
rejecting a nominal run (i.e.~assuming the camera signal follows the above 
distribution) based on this selection criterion is approximately 
$\SI{1.7}{\percent}$.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/fd_event_sum.png}
  \label{fig:fd-event-sum}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/calib_constant_distribution.png}
  \label{fig:calib-distribution}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:fd-event-sum} The distribution of camera signal 
  recorded over all runs. \subref{fig:calib-distribution} Distribution of 
  measured XY pixel calibration constants for accepted and rejected scans.}
  \label{fig:xy-qa-results}
\end{figure}

\subsection{First Production Dataset}
\label{ssec:xy-production-dataset}

We have presented an extensive list of quality checks by which the quality of XY
measurement data can be tested. In conclusion, eight runs out of a total of 66 
runs (\SI{12}{\percent}) are rejected. Analyzing the distribution of 
accepted/rejected calibration constants, also shown in 
\cref{fig:calib-distribution}, we find 

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:xy-qa-results}
\upsigma\left(C^\mathrm{XY}_\mathrm{abs.}\right)\bigg|_\mathrm{rej.} = 2.1131\,\frac{\upgamma}{\ADC},\qquad
\upsigma\left(C^\mathrm{XY}_\mathrm{abs.}\right)\bigg|_\mathrm{acc.} = 0.4992\,\frac{\upgamma}{\ADC}.
\end{equation}

Notably, this decrease in the standard deviation of measured calibration 
constants is achieved without analyzing the calibration constants themselves. 
This implies that the quality cuts are able to correctly identify sub-optimal 
measurement conditions, which are in turn correlated to outlier calibration 
constants.

With almost all XY-Scans conducted in Nov.~2024 passing all quality checks 
(Ref.~\cref{tab:xy-runlist}), we choose this campaign as the first official set
of calibration constants to be used for \FD reconstruction. The two runs that do
not pass the quality assurance ($\#16461$, and $\#6522$) are replaced with 
$\#15675$ (\LL3, Oct.~2022) and $\#6070$ (\HEAT1, Nov.~2023) respectively. The 
entire database, consisting of 11880 XY pixel calibration constants over all 27
\FD telescopes is accessible under 
\cite{filipAugerobservatorySandBoxesPaulfilip2026}.

\section{Telescope Ageing}
\label{sec:telescope-aging}

The selection criteria defined in \cref{sec:quality-assurance} ensure that the
XY-Scanner delivers high-quality pixel calibration constants. The most urgent 
follow-up question is how do the recent XY measurements compare to the drum
measurements.

With more than a decade lying between both absolute calibrations, differences in
the telescope operation (and $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$) are expected. Not
only is the XY-Scanner thus valuable in and of itself due to its measurement 
data, but also serves as an important tool to understand and check the relative 
calibration tracking algorithms.

\subsection{Comparison of Drum and XY}
\label{ssec:drum-xy-comparison}

As mentioned before, differences in the comparison of the absolute calibration
constants are expected, primarily due to the calibration sets having been 
measured at different times, and the \FD telescopes ageing in between.

Therefore, a more interesting comparison is that of the  relative calibration 
constants, calculated from tracking the drum over time with the Calibration A, 
with the XY-Scanner. These datasets represent a (theoretical) knowledge of the
detector at the same point in time. \cref{fig:xy-vs-drum} shows this comparison.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/drum_vs_xy.png}
  \caption{Comparison of the drum calibration constants versus the XY 
  calibration constants. The drum calibration is propagated to the time of the
  XY-Scan via the relative tracking of the Calibration A signal. The XY dataset
  in use defined in \cref{ssec:xy-production-dataset}.}
  \label{fig:xy-vs-drum}
\end{figure}

Large discrepancies of up to \SI{40}{\percent} are evident in the telescope 
mean. While the exact magnitude differs from site to site and telescope to 
telescope, the general trend is 
$C^\mathrm{XY}_\mathrm{abs} > C^\mathrm{Drum}_\mathrm{abs}$ for almost all 
pixels in the \FD. Clearly, the pixel calibration constants measured by the two
different methods are not in agreement.

This discrepancy is not completely unexpected, albeit the amplitude certainly
is. Evidences of a sub-optimal understanding of the \FD telescope ageing have
been collected over time, but were partially attibuted to e.g.~software bugs 
\cite{dawsonCommentDriftFD2013}, or variations in the solar cycle 
\cite{newmannNightSkyBrightness2014}. In an attempt to address these puzzles, a
systematic uncertainty of approximately \SI{9}{\percent} has been added to the 
pixel calibration constants measured by the drum in the past 
(Ref.~\cref{tab:xy-systematics}).

A closer look at the calibration constants of individual telescopes, as shown in
\cref{fig:closer-look} sheds more light on the nature of the discrepancy. The 
values $C^\mathrm{Drum}_\mathrm{abs}$ show a narrow distribution, with the 
pixel-\PMTs having been flat-fielded just before the Drum measurement 
\cite{dorofeevFDAbsoluteCalibration}. 14 years later, the XY measurements
indicate that both the mean and standard deviation of the distribution has 
increased. This is consistent with the assumption that as the telescope ages it 
becomes less sensitive to light. Different pixels in the camera seem to age at 
different rates.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/CO4_comparison.png}
  \label{fig:xy-drum-cala-1}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.38\textwidth]{xy-scanner/LL2_comparison.png}
  \label{fig:xy-drum-cala-2}
  }
  \caption[]{The comparison of calibration constants for 
  \subref{fig:xy-drum-cala-1} \CO4 and \subref{fig:xy-drum-cala-2} \LL2. Shown 
  are the distributions for the drum measured in 2010 (red), XY measured in 2024  (black), and drum tracked to 2024 (blue).}
  \label{fig:closer-look}
\end{figure}

The Calibration A tracks the increase in standard deviation accurately, but 
fails to recognize the shift in the mean of the distribution. As a result, the 
Calibration A sees a decrease in the value of some calibration constants,
seemingly implying that some pixels in the camera become more sensitive as time 
goes on. This shortcoming is the reason for the discrepancies visible in 
\cref{fig:xy-vs-drum}. 

With the Calibration A only tracking the sensitivity of the pixel-\PMTs 
(Ref.~\cref{sssec:relative-calibration}) the shift in the distribution mean must
originate from a telescope component that is not the camera. It is thus evident 
that the evolution of the entire optical path in the detector must be considered 
in order to accurately extrapolate the \FD sensitivity from one moment in time 
to another.

\subsection{Effects Of Mirror Cleaning}
\label{ssec:effects-of-mirror-cleaning}

The \FD is located in a semi-arid environment that is at times dominated by 
strong winds (called the Zonda \cite{municipalidadmalargueVientoZondaSitio}) 
driven by the Andes. Consequentially, dust steadily contaminates the \FD 
buildings and settles on mirrors and filters, negatively affecting their 
performance.

The telescope filters are regularly cleaned since 2014, and mirror cleaning 
operations are carried out to combat this. Notably, two interventions have been 
accompanied by XY-Scans before and after the cleaning. The comparison of these
measurements provide valuable information in the identification of how and why 
the detector is ageing in a way that is invisible to the Calibration A.

\subsubsection{Los Morados 1-6, 2025}
\label{sssec:lm-cleaning}

All mirrors in \LM have been cleaned in November of 2025, following almost ten 
years of uninterrupted pollution by dust. All other \FD sites have been cleaned
(semi-) regularly with a higher frequency \cite{MergedListOfCleaningsAugerWiki},
meaning that \LM likely presents the most extreme case of dust contamination, 
and we anticipate the most drastic changes in telescope operation before and 
after cleaning.

With only days passing between the initial XY-Scan, mirror cleaning, and final
XY-Scan, the changes in $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ predicted from both measurements 
must, of course, originate largely from the altered reflectivity of the mirror 
segments. We normalize the XY signals to the respective camera sensitivity, 
which is given by the mean signal response to the Calibration A measurement 
before and after the scan. This is analog to the normalizations applied on 
quality cuts in \cref{sec:quality-assurance}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/mirror_cleaning_lm1.png}
  \caption{Comparison of XY signal strength before and after mirror cleaning for
  \LM1, normalized to the respective Calibration A signal. The left subplot 
  shows the aperture view, and the center subplot gives the camera view.}
  \label{fig:lm1-cleaning}
\end{figure}

\cref{fig:lm1-cleaning} shows the comparison of the corrected XY signal, 
labelled $S^*$, before and after mirror cleaning, both per flash position 
(averaged over the entire camera) and per pixel (averaged over all flashes). 
Several facts are evident:

\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{itemize}
\item The camera receives more light after mirror cleaning.
\item The gain in light collection efficiency is elevation dependent.
\item Light collection from beyond the aperture sharply decreases.
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.2cm}

All these findings can be completely understood by analyzing changes in the
mirror reflectivity post cleaning. First, and most obvious, clean(er) mirrors 
reflect more light. This results in more light reaching the \FD-camera, and a 
larger signal recorded in the pixel-\PMTs. The calibration constants increase.

Specifically, the specular reflectivity of the mirrors drastically increases,
whereas the diffuse reflection (stemming primarily from light scattering off of
dust particles on the mirror surface) diminishes. This can be seen most 
prevalently by comparing the light signal received from flasher positions 
outside of the aperture in \cref{fig:lm1-cleaning}. There exists no geometric 
path\footnote{Ignoring trajectories involving multiple scattering.} for which 
light reaches the \FD-camera by specularly reflecting from the mirror. Light 
emitted from beyond the aperture must thus be reflected diffusely into the 
camera. With $S^*_\mathrm{after}/S^*_\mathrm{before} < 1$, this implies the 
diffuse reflectivity of the mirror was higher before mirror cleaning. This is 
verified by independent measurements of the mirror segment reflectivity 
\cite{filipJumpsXYCalibration2024, GAP2026_001DescriptionResults}.

Finally, the gain in light collection efficiency is not uniform over the
camera. Gradients in the positive $y$-direction are visible in 
\cref{fig:lm1-cleaning}. The average gain per row (Ref.~\cref{fig:lm-gradient}) 
grows steadily from $\approx\SI{5}{\percent}$ at the top of the camera to 
$\approx\SI{15}{\percent}$ near the bottom of the camera. This results from the
inclination of the \FD mirrors (Ref.~\cref{fig:fd-schematic}). The bottom 
segments are more inclined and offer a better surface for dust to cling on to.
In contrast, the top of the mirror is less contaminated.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/lm_gradient.png}
  \caption{The gain in light collection efficiency, averaged over all pixels in
  a row. Low row numbers are located at the top of the camera, and look at the 
  top of the mirrors.}
  \label{fig:lm-gradient}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{HEAT 1+3, 2023}
\label{sssec:heat-mirror-cleaning}

The mirrors of the \HEAT are located in an enclosure that differs from the 
standard \FD-building. The altered design makes the mirrors more susceptible to
dust accumulation. Despite this difference, the gain in light collection
efficiency in \HEAT 1+2 after a mirror cleaning operation in November 2023 
(Ref.~\cref{fig:he1-cleaning}) is on average $\SI{15}{\percent}$, and very 
similar to the jump visible in \cref{sssec:lm-cleaning}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.42\textwidth, valign=t]{xy-scanner/mirror_cleaning_he3.png}
  \label{fig:he1-cleaning}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth, valign=t]{xy-scanner/malargue_wind_rose_cropped.png}
  \label{fig:he2-location}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:he1-cleaning} Comparison of XY signal strength before
  and after mirror cleaning for \HEAT1, normalized to the respective Calibration
  A signal. \subref{fig:he2-location} Sattelite image of the \HEATs with wind
  occurence and strength overlayed. \HEAT2 (center, red) is shielded from the 
  main wind directions. Sattelite image from \cite{GoogleMaps}, weather data 
  from \cite{akrherzIowaEnvironmentalMesonet}.}
  \label{fig:heat-cleaning}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{HEAT 2, 2023}
\label{sssec:he2-problem}

\HEAT 2 was cleaned during the same November 2023 campaign, but showed a jump in
calibration constants and light collection efficiency of just \SI{8}{\percent}, 
much smaller than \HEAT1 and \HEAT2. Auxiliary measurements of the mirror 
reflectivity have turned up similar findings \cite{filipJumpsXYCalibration2024}.
The simplest explanation for this abnormal behaviour is the fact that \HEAT2 was
not as dirty as \HEAT1+3. This is supported by an analysis of the pixel-\PMT
variance in the telescopes, shown in \cref{fig:he-nsb}.

All telescopes show an increase in the trace variance after the cleaning of the
mirrors, meaning that $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC}$ must be a proxy for the \FD
camera sensitivity. Expanding from this logic, we find that the variance in 
\HEAT2 is higher (than \HEAT1+3), meaning that the \HEAT2 camera must have been 
more sensitive prior to mirror cleaning.

A look at the geographic conditions at \HEAT also yields one possible 
explanation for this observation. \cref{fig:he2-location} shows that the 
\HEAT1+3 telescopes shield \HEAT2 from the most prevalent wind directions.

\section{The Night Sky Background}
\label{sec:night-sky-background}

The previous section has shown that the \FD trace variances,
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC}$, can be used as a first-order estimate of the 
pixel-\PMT sensitivity, and have been analyzed in the past to verify the 
calibration parameters of the drum \cite{GAP2016_005CrossCheck}. The trace 
variance is monitored with minute accuracy (Ref.~\cref{ssec:fd-triggers}) while 
the \PMT \HV is turned on. Meanwhile, the Calibration A, being the standard 
relative tracking algorithm for the time being, is measured only twice per 
night.

On top of this, the Calibration A is only sensitive to changes in the 
pixel-\PMTs, offering an incomplete view of the telescope response function.
This has caused for a divergence of the calibration constants that are in use
versus those that have been measured (Ref.~\cref{ssec:drum-xy-comparison}). The 
trace variances, on the other hand, depend on the entire optical path and 
signal readout chain, they are an end-to-end estimator of the complete 
telescope sensitivity, involving e.g.~changes in the mirror reflectivity.

In the following, we aim to derive an alternative relative tracking algorithm 
to the (insufficient) Calibration A method using this extensive dataset.

\subsection{Preprocessing of Data}
\label{ssec:preprocessing-of-data}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/nsb_shift_example.png}
  \caption{Evolution of the trace variance for pixel $\#210$ in \CO4 in October
  2020. The right subplot gives a \KDE of all data points during the shift.}
  \label{fig:nsb-shift-example}
\end{figure}

\cref{fig:nsb-shift-example} shows the evolution of the trace variance for one 
pixel over one \FD measurement shift (also called moon cycle), typically 
consisting of 14 to 21 nights of \DAQ. The moon cycle starts with a waning 
crescent moon and ends after waxing crescent. The residual moonlight can cause 
a strong evolution of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC}$ in the first and last measuremen 
nights, whereas an almost constant variance is being recorded in the middle of 
the \FD shift during new moon.

Several other aspects influence the value of the trace variance. Most 
importantly, $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC}\neq0$ even during supposed total darkness 
at the beginning and end of each \DAQ night, when the \FD shutters are closed. 
This is due to electronic noise, $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{elec.}$, in the \PMTs, 
averaging roughly $\SI{4}{\mathrm{ADC}\squared}$, and visible as a large spike 
in the \acf{KDE} visible on the right in \cref{fig:nsb-shift-example}.

We estimate $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{elec.}$ as the maximum of the shift-wise \KDE of 
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC}$ per pixel, and calculate the \NSB by subtracting

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:night-sky-background}
\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB} = \upsigma^2_\mathrm{ADC} - \upsigma^2_\mathrm{elec.}.
\end{equation}

This \NSB is a minute accurate estimate of the fluctuations in light entering 
the pixel-\PMTs. The light in question stems not only from \EASs, but
airglow, stars, zodiacal light, as well as galactic glow 
\cite{GAP2016_005CrossCheck}. All these phenomena are heavily dependent on the 
terrestrial as well as solar weather conditions, and their evolution for a 
given night is thus difficult to model. To mitigate the influence of 
(short-scale) weather phenomena and compromise temporal resolution and data 
volume, we use the hourly median of the \NSB to conduct our subsequent 
analysis. 

The resulting dataset spans 15 years from $2010/01/01$ (shortly before the 
absolute calibration with the drum) until $2025/12/31$, covering almost 
$22\,000$ hours of \DAQ for the $11880$ individuals pixels in all $27$ 
\FD-telescopes. 

\subsection{Recurrent Large Scale Patterns}
\label{ssec:large-scale-patterns}

The temporal evolution of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ for individual pixels over 
the past 15 years is shown in \cref{fig:one-pixel-nsb}. The \NSB dataset 
remains noisy ($\frac{\upsigma}{\upmu} = \frac{8.8}{20.4} \approx 
\SI{43}{\percent}$), even at an hourly resolution. Calculating a rolling 
average over data over a month yields a clearer image and reveals seasonal 
trends.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/one_pixel.png}
  \label{fig:one-pixel-nsb}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/one_pixel_psd.png}
  \label{fig:one-pixel-psd}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:one-pixel-nsb} \NSB data spanning 15 years for 
  different pixels in the \CO4 camera. \subref{fig:one-pixel-psd} Lomb-Scargle
  periodogram of the data in \subref{fig:one-pixel-nsb}. The difference in
  pulse amplitudes among pixels stems from the difference in the mean 
  $\upmu_\mathrm{NSB}$. When accounting for this difference the magnitude of
  the \FD ageing is the same for all pixels.}
  \label{fig:one-pixel-view}
\end{figure}

These seasonal trends and other large scale patterns become even more evident 
when calculating a Lomb-Scargle periodogram 
\cite{lombLeastsquaresFrequencyAnalysis1976,scargleStudiesAstronomicalTime1982}
from the irregularly sampled \NSB. \cref{fig:one-pixel-psd} shows the \PSD, it 
is evident that several effects contribute to the erratic \NSB patterns

\subsubsection{Nightly Variations}
\label{sssec:nightly-variations}

The strongest influence over the minute-value of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ hold
short-scale effects with period durations of $\leq\SI{1}{\mathrm{day}}$. These
effects are weather phenomena (cloud occlusion, lightning, star transitions, 
etc.) and the nightly evolution of the telescope sensitivity driven by changes 
in the electronic gain of the pixel-\PMTs.

With the former especially being dependent on (to the \FD) external variables, 
the aforementioned effects are difficult to model. Their amplitude is 
indicative of the accuracy with which one can predict the temporal evolution of
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$.

\subsubsection{Moon Cycles}

Several modulations with period lengths of $\SIrange{25}{32}{\mathrm{days}}$ 
are visible in \cref{fig:one-pixel-psd}. These stem from the \FD duty cycle, 
which is synchronized to the lunar phases such that \DAQ always occurs between 
waning and waxing crescent (Ref.~\cref{ssec:preprocessing-of-data}).

This change from crescent to new moon and back influences the ambient 
brightness of the night sky, and is left imprinted on $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ 
as monthly modulations.

\subsubsection{Seasonal Patterns}

The night side of the Earth is exposed to different parts of the night sky as
it revolves around the sun. This results in a strong yearly modulation of
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$. The fluctuations are amplified by seasonally recurring
atmospheric conditions.

The second harmonic of this modulation is also well visible at a period length
of six months, and possibly reinforced by the half-wave symmetry of the
terrestrial orbit creating similar conditions for air glow and zodiacal light 
bi-annually. Higher order harmonics are not (clearly) visible in the \PSD, most
likely due to the strongly decorrelating effects of nightly variations.

\subsubsection{Sinusoidal Model}
\label{sssec:sinusoidal-model}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/sinusoidal_model.png}
  \caption{Construction of an empirical model (red line) of the \NSB for 
  pixel \#210 in \CO4 (black dots). The top two subplots show the seasonal 
  fluctuations of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$, and the bottom subplot adds the 
  modulations from moon cycles.}
  \label{fig:sinusoidal-model}
\end{figure}

An empirical model of the \NSB temporal evolution can be derived from the 
periodically recurrent effects discussed above. Beginning with the mean value 
$\langle \upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}\rangle$ per pixel, averaged over the entire 
time range, an approximate value of the \NSB at $t$ days after 2010-01-01 can 
be derived from a Fourier-like series as

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fourier-components}
f(t) = \langle \upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB} \rangle + \sum\limits_{\{k\}}\,a_{k}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{T_k} t\right) + b_{k}\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{T_k} t\right).
\end{equation}

\begin{wraptable}{r}{0.46\textwidth}
\begingroup
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.0}
\caption{Coefficients for $f(t)$ in \cref{fig:sinusoidal-model}.}
	\begin{tabular}{cccc}
  	\toprule
	\hline
	$k$ & $T\,/\,\mathrm{days}$ & $a_k\,/\,\ADC^2$ & $b_k\,/\,\ADC^2$ \\
	\hline
	$1$ & $362.26$ & $3.3648$ & $1.3141$ \\
	$2$ & $182.95$ & $-0.8216$ & $-2.6550$ \\
	\hline
	$3$ & $32.14$ & $0.9096$ & $2.0422$ \\
	$4$ & $29.62$ & $3.3568$ & $0.5773$ \\
	$5$ & $27.31$ & $1.2418$ & $-1.6838$ \\
	$6$ & $25.42$ & $-2.1036$ & $0.9739$ \\
  	\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
	\label{tab:sinusoidal-model}
\endgroup
\end{wraptable}

This is done for pixel \#210 in \CO4, where 
$\langle\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}\rangle = \SI{20.3959}{\square\ADC}$. The 
resulting model is displayed in \cref{fig:sinusoidal-model}, and the 
coefficients of $f(t)$ are listed in \cref{tab:sinusoidal-model}. 

It is important to note that this model is valid for pixel \#210 only. Since 
other pixels have different pointing directions they monitor different areas of
the night sky, which will evolve differently throughout the seasons. $a_k$ and 
$b_k$ in \cref{tab:sinusoidal-model}, as a result, do not generalize over all 
pixels in a camera, let alone over different telescopes. Similar resonances at
the natural frequencies of the \NSB are expected (and observed in 
\cref{fig:one-pixel-psd}), however.

\subsubsection{Model Performance}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/model_benchmark_Coihueco_4.png}
  \caption{Comparison of the sinusoidal model derived in 
  \cref{sssec:sinusoidal-model} to the \NSB data of different pixels in \CO4. 
  The hollow points measured on Nov. 19th 2024 are extracted from XY scans. For
  details refer to \cref{sssec:nsb-during-xy-scans}.}
  \label{fig:nsb-model-comparison}
\end{figure}

We benchmark the sinusoidal parametrization derived in 
\cref{sssec:sinusoidal-model} by comparing the model trend against measured 
\NSB data. This is done in \cref{fig:one-pixel-nsb} for the entire dataset 
spanning 15 years, and, to provide a more detailed look, in 
\cref{fig:nsb-model-comparison} for one moon cycle close to an XY-Scanner 
measurement. The latter plot especially displays the limits of the Fourier 
expansion. The model estimates the variance level during the \DAQ period 
accurately, but cannot predict $\upsigma_\mathrm{NSB}^2$ on a given night.

Nightly variations are deliberately omitted during the construction of the 
model, and could perhaps increase the predictive power. With their evolution 
being heavily dependent on local weather conditions however (and thus chaotic 
rather than recurrent), the insights gathered by building a Fourier expansion 
from non-recurrent patterns are questionable.

%Moving to longer timeframes, we see that the sinusoidal model in 
%\cref{fig:sinusoidal-model} tends to overestimate $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ for
%data after 2019. This is evidence to the telescope ageing discussed in  
%\cref{sec:telescope-aging}, and also visible at the tail-end of the \PSD in 
%\cref{fig:one-pixel-psd}.

\subsection{Non-Recurrent Evolution}

One final feature in \cref{fig:one-pixel-psd} remains undiscussed. The \PSD 
shows a high amplitude for frequencies just barely measureable in the 15 year 
window of available data. The spike persists after applying windowing 
functions, and thus cannot (only) be a computational artifact. Instead, this 
result points to an ageing of the \FD.

The simplest parametrization to model the non-recurrent contribution to the 
long-term evolution of the \NSB is that of a constant drift. Physically, this
might correspond to the continuous accumulation of dirt on the \FD mirror and 
filter. 

We estimate the magnitude of this possible drift by fitting a first-order 
polynomial to the time-dependent $\upsigma_\mathrm{NSB}$, hoping that all 
(recurrent or short-scale) modulations discussed in 
\cref{ssec:large-scale-patterns} balance out when analyzing the entire dataset.

In order to be as agnostic as possible to other forms of ageing, we estimate 
the telescope drift on a per-month level by aggregating two years of \NSB data
before and after a given month (comprising four years in total) and performing 
the linear fit on this sub-sample. Examples of this are shown in 
\cref{fig:rolling-window-drift}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/rolling_window_drift.png}
  \caption{Time dependency of the slope in the linear fit to the \NSB dataset
  in pixel \#199 in \LM1 (black) and \#210 in \CO4 (red). The dashed lines give
  a smoothed trend.}
  \label{fig:rolling-window-drift}
\end{figure}	

The evident time-dependency of the fitted slope immediately call the assumption
of a constant drift into question. While a (linear?) drift does seem to be 
inherent to $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$, the rate of ageing cannot be constant.

Worse still, the temporal evolution in \cref{fig:rolling-window-drift} look 
similar, but in detail are site- and pixel-dependent. Using a \FIR 
Savitzky-Golay filter with window length 24 months and polynomial order of one,
we can coarsely subdivide the datasets into epochs where the drift magnitude is
decreasing, stable, or increasing. The reasons for these trends may be 
multifaceted. A list of candidate causes (without any order of importance or 
significance) can be found below:

\begingroup
\renewcommand\arraystretch{2}
\vspace{0.2cm}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Antrophic light pollution}: Oil companies are constructing 
refineries in the vicinity of Malargüe \cite{mineroSeguroFlagshipProject2026}. 
Their light imprint at night is visible from the \FD sites, and cause for a 
step increase in the median $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ over time.

\item \textbf{Mirror/filter contamination}: The mirror reflectivity and 
\UV-filter transmissivity is directly anti-correlated to the amount of dust, 
dirt and aerosols on the respective surface. As both components become more 
contaminated less light from outside of the aperture reaches the \FD-camera, 
and $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ decreases.

\item \textbf{Mirror/filter cleaning}: As shown in 
\cref{ssec:effects-of-mirror-cleaning} the above trend can be counteracted by
cleaning the \FD mirrors and filters. This results in an instantaneous increase
of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ of the order $\mathcal{O}(\SI{10}{\percent})$, 
possibly followed by a longer period of larger decrease as the mirrors quickly 
become contaminated again. Only the instantaneous rise has been investigated in
the scope of this thesis.

\item \textbf{PMT ageing}: The performance of the pixel-\PMTs is not constant 
with time. Tracking the measured signal of Calibration A runs over the years
reveals that the quantum efficiency of the \PMTs decreases. This directly leads
to a decrease of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ over time.

\item \textbf{Electronic response}: \todo[inline]{ask gaetano about this?}

\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.2cm}
\endgroup

\subsubsection{Global NSB Model}

Though common patterns like a leveling of the \NSB drift around the beginning
of 2015 is visible in many telescopes, and may be connected to the beginning of
regular filter cleaning campaigns, the identification of not only different 
trends but also their cause in all \FD pixels is a tremendous task.

Moreover, given the large daily fluctuations present in the \NSB it is 
questionable whether this identification is even possible on the full dataset
as it is presented here. Quality cuts that remove cloud occlusion 
\cite{tairliCloudDetectionUsing2025}, or limit the \NSB samples to transitions
of stars with well-known brightness \todo{cite} allow for a more detailed 
analysis of each pixel-\PMT. This shall lay outside of the scope of this work 
though.

Instead, we assume that the \FD ageing is well described by the simple model 
mentioned above, namely, a drift with (negative) constant slope in 
$\upsigma_\mathrm{NSB}$ over the entire dataset. 

\todo[inline]{standardize calibration constants}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:backtracking-constants}
\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}(t) = m_\mathrm{NSB}(t-t_\mathrm{ref}) \cdot \upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}(t_\mathrm{ref}) 
\end{equation}

The magnitude of this drift is shown in detail for one pixel, across two 
cameras, and the entire \FD dataset in \cref{fig:camera-drift}. Moving forward,
we assume that $m_\mathrm{NSB}$ is equal for all pixels, and is given by the 
mean of a Gaussian fit made over the entire dataset of pixel drifts. It 
measures

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:drift-size}
	m_\mathrm{NSB} = \SI{-1.97\pm0.01}{\percent\per\year}.
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/pixel_210.png}
  \label{fig:co4-210-pixel-drift}
  }\hfill
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{xy-scanner/Coihueco4_camera_drift.png}
  \label{fig:co4-camera-drift}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.47\textwidth]{xy-scanner/LosMorados1_camera_drift.png}
  \label{fig:lm1-camera-dri}
  }\hfill
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/all_drift.png}
  \label{fig:all-drift}
  }
  \caption[]{The magnitude of $m_\mathrm{NSB}$ (measured in percent per year) 
  shown in detail for \subref{fig:co4-210-pixel-drift} one pixel, across two
  \FD cameras in \subref{fig:co4-camera-drift} and \subref{fig:lm1-camera-dri},
  and for all \FD detectors in \subref{fig:all-drift}. No significant elevation
  dependence (i.e.~dependence on camera row number) is visible in the bottom 
  three figures. The color gradient in \subref{fig:all-drift} is built across 
  all telescopes. Conclusions on and comparisons to the individual telescope 
  histograms are erroneous.}
  \label{fig:camera-drift}
\end{figure}

\newpage

\section{FD Energy Scale}
\label{sec:fd-energy-scale}

\todo[inline]{standardize calibration A}

We established that the pixel calibration constants $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$
in use in the current \FD event reconstruction software
(Ref.~\cref{ssec:fd-reconstruction}) disagree with recent measurements made by
the XY-Scanner due to the relative tracking algorithm failing to account for
the ageing of vital telescope components like the mirrors or \UV-filter.

In this section we offer an alternative tracking algorithm for the 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$, and analyze how reconstructed shower energies change when 
employing the updated calibration constants in the event reconstruction.

\subsection{Alternative Calibration Tracking}

The current \FD calibration tracking algorithm, which employs the signal 
strength $S_\mathrm{LCU}$ of the calibration A light source, as well as the 
detected signal $S_\mathrm{CalA}$ in the telescope camera, is given in 
\cref{eq:cal-a-propagation}. Naively, this algorithm can be amended to
introduce an end-to-end tracker such as the \NSB, heuristically

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:nsb-tracking}
C^i_\mathrm{abs} = C^i_\mathrm{ref}\,\cdot\,\frac{\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB}\,\mathrm{ref}}}{\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB}}}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Determination of reference variance level}
\label{sssec:nsb-during-xy-scans}

$\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB}\,\mathrm{ref}}$ in \cref{eq:nsb-tracking} is the 
reference \NSB signal at the time of the XY-Scanner measurements of 
$C^i_\mathrm{ref}$, and is not digitized during the altered \DAQ procedure. 

Rather than relying on preexisting database entries, we must hence estimate the
reference night sky background. One method to do so is to analyze the 
pixel-\PMT traces of the XY-Scan. The trace variance in the portion of the 
digitized light flashes prior to the signal pulse serves as a proxy for 
$\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB}\,\mathrm{ref}}$. 

Concretely, for every pixel we take the first $n_\mathrm{start}-10$ bins of 
each trace, where $n_\mathrm{start}$ gives the start bin of the \CLS flash as 
estimated by the pulse finder algorithm 
(Ref.~\cite[Section 7.1.1]{schaferXYScannerVersatileMethod2023}), and calculate
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{start}$. A typical trace showing
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{start}$ is shown in \cref{fig:xy-example-trace}. In doing 
this for all pixels and XY-Scans, we note strong temporal evolutions in 
$\upsigma_\mathrm{start}$ for some pixels as shown in \cref{fig:star-transit}, 
or even the entire camera in extreme cases.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.44\textwidth]{xy-scanner/star_transition.png}
  \label{fig:star-transit}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.44\textwidth]{xy-scanner/nsb_ref_level.png}
  \label{fig:nsb-ref}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:star-transit} Temporal evolution of the trace variance
  for an example XY-Scan. A star transits through the \FOV of the highlighted 
  pixels during the measurement. \subref{fig:nsb-ref} 
  $\langle \upsigma^2_\mathrm{start}\rangle$ for all pixels in
  \subref{fig:star-transit}. Nightly variations are effectively filtered out.}
  \label{fig:nsb-ref-plots}
\end{figure}

These short-term effects ranging from star transitions to weather effects are 
the nightly variations of the \NSB as introduced in 
\cref{sssec:nightly-variations}, and which make it so difficult to build a 
cohesive model of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$. We attempt to limit the effects of
nightly variations by estimating $\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB}\,\mathrm{ref}} = 
\langle \upsigma^2_{\mathrm{start}} \rangle$ on a subportion of
traces for which $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{start}$ is approximately uniform between 
\CLS flashes. For details see \todo{appendix}.

This trace level analysis yields an estimate of the reference variance level as
shown in \cref{fig:nsb-model-comparison} and \cref{fig:nsb-ref} for example 
XY-Scanner measurements.

With $\upsigma^2_{\mathrm{NSB},\,\mathrm{ref}}$ at hand it is trivial to 
perform a (back) tracking of $C^i_\mathrm{ref}$, as done in for one pixel in
\cref{fig:nsb-propagation}. Two key insights can be gathered from this
exercise:

\vspace{0.4cm}
\begin{itemize}
\item The noisyness of $\upsigma^2_\mathrm{NSB}$ renders \cref{eq:nsb-tracking}
useless to precisely estimate $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$.
\item Drum and XY-Scanner measurements are compatible when using an end-to-end tracking variable such as the pixel-\PMT variance.
\end{itemize}
\vspace{0.4cm}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/nsb_propagation.png}
  \caption{Comparison of Calibration A tracked drum calibration constants and 
  $C^\mathrm{XY}_\mathrm{abs}$ from \CO4 pixel \#210, tracked according to 
  \cref{eq:nsb-tracking}. The reference XY-Scan is marked as a red dot. The
  lines help visualize the long-term-trend in the calibration bias.}
  \label{fig:nsb-propagation}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Constant drift model}
\label{sssec:constant-drift-model}

We propose a model that mitigates the influence of nightly variations in 
$\upsigma^2_\mathrm{ref}$ via assuming a constant drift of $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ 
analog to \cref{eq:backtracking-constants}, namely

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:calib-constant-tracking}
C^i_\mathrm{abs}(t) = m_\mathrm{NSB}(t-t_\mathrm{ref}) \cdot C^i_\mathrm{ref}.
\end{equation}

Using \cref{eq:calib-constant-tracking}, we can calculate a set of calibration
constants that are completely independent of any measurement information
involved in the standard tracking algorithm employing the Drum \CLS and the 
Calibration A. A comparison between both models is shown in 
\cref{fig:constant-drift-tracking}. Importantly, the measured 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ from Drum \CLS and XY-Scanner are compatible using this 
tracking algorithm as well, while the noise introduced from nightly variations
in the \NSB is eliminated.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/constant_drift_tracking.png}
  \caption{Comparison of the (standard) tracking algorithm using the Drum \CLS 
  and Calibration A versus backpropagation with the XY-Scanner measurements and
  a constant drift measured from the \NSB according to 
  \cref{eq:calib-constant-tracking}.}
  \label{fig:constant-drift-tracking}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Effects on Reconstructed Energy}

Lastly, we use \cref{eq:calib-constant-tracking} to update the calibration
constants during an \FD reconstruction procedure and compare the change in 
estimated primary energy by switching from the standard energy scale 
(i.e.~Drum \CLS and Calibration A) to the XY-Scanner energy scale. Of 
particular interest is the quantity $E_\mathrm{SD} / E_\mathrm{FD}$ for hybrid
events.\footnote{Hybrid events are events for which \SD and \FD information 
exists.}

Whereas a value of $\langle E_\mathrm{SD}/E_\mathrm{FD}\rangle\approx1$ is 
observed for events recorded around 2010\footnote{Which is expected given that
the Drum measurements were taken in January 2010.} in 
\cref{fig:e-fd-drift-cala} later dates are distinguished by a small positive 
bias that increases with time at a magnitude of \SI{1.01}{\percent}. This 
instability of the energy \SD/\FD energy estimators has been noted and 
investigated in the past \cite{GAP2016_005CrossCheck, dawsonCommentDriftFD2013,
newmannNightSkyBrightness2014}.

Studies of the \SD event rate above fixed energies have indicated that the \SD 
reconstruction delivers an approximately stable energy estimator 
\cite{schmidtSDRateEnergy2026}. As a consequence, the observed drift must be 
explained by an evolution of $E_\mathrm{FD}$ over time. 

\cref{fig:e-fd-drift-nsb} shows the ratio of energies for events reconstructed 
using $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ from \cref{eq:calib-constant-tracking}. Not only is
the trend in $E_\mathrm{SD}/E_\mathrm{FD}$ reversed, the magnitude of the trend
is greatly reduced. By employing an, albeit rudimentary, end-to-end calibration
tracking algorithm we can effectively address the drift of the \FD energy 
estimator.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/e_ratio_std_calib.png}
  \label{fig:e-fd-drift-cala}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/e_ratio_nsb.png}
  \label{fig:e-fd-drift-nsb}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:e-fd-drift-cala} $E_\mathrm{SD}/E_\mathrm{FD}$ for 
  reconstructed hybrid events from 2008 to 2025. The decreases in the ratio 
  around 2015 and 2024 stem from mirror cleaning campaigns at \CO and \HEAT 
  (Ref.~\cref{ssec:heat-mirror-cleaning}). \subref{fig:e-fd-drift-nsb} 
  $E_\mathrm{SD}/E_\mathrm{FD}$ for the same set of observed shower events, but
  reconstructed using $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ from 
  \cref{eq:calib-constant-tracking}.}
  \label{fig:e-fd-drift}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{SD Energy Calibration}

One detail of \cref{fig:e-fd-drift-nsb} must be discussed; The \SD energy scale
is cross-calibrated to match the \FD energy scale. Since the change in 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ manifests in a systematic positive bias in the \FD energy, 
the parameters in \cref{eq:energy-cross-calibration} and 
\cref{tab:energy-cross-calibration} must be revisited. We follow the procedure
detailed in \cite{aabMeasurementCosmicrayEnergy2020} to optimize $A$, and $B$ 
on a subset of hybrid events which pass extensive quality cuts 
(Ref.~\cite{ravignaniMeasurementCosmicrayEnergy2025}). In doing this for both
datasets of reconstructed showers in \cref{fig:e-fd-drift} we find

\begin{align}
\label{eq:drum-energy-scale}
A^\mathrm{Drum} &= \SI{14.3\pm0.3}{\peta\eV},\qquad B^\mathrm{Drum} = 0.983\pm0.006, \\
\label{eq:xy-energy-scale}
A^\mathrm{XY} &= \SI{15.4\pm0.4}{\peta\eV},\qquad B^\mathrm{XY} = 0.995\pm0.006.
\end{align}

The respective calibration curves are shown in \cref{fig:e-cal}. The 
$E_\mathrm{SD}$ in \cref{fig:e-fd-drift} have been calculated using the 
respective best fit parameters.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/PhaseI_standard.png}
  \label{fig:e-cal-standard}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{xy-scanner/PhaseI_nsb_drift.png}
  \label{fig:e-cal-nsb}
  }\hfill
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{xy-scanner/calib_comparison.png}
  \label{fig:calib-comparison}
  }
  \caption[]{\SD energy cross calibration for events reconstructed with 
  $C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ from \subref{fig:e-cal-standard} the drum and Calibration 
  A, and \subref{fig:e-cal-nsb} the XY-Scanner
  (Ref.~\cref{sssec:constant-drift-model}). \subref{fig:calib-comparison} Ratio
  of \SD energies reconstructed from \cref{eq:drum-energy-scale} and 
  \cref{eq:xy-energy-scale}.}
  \label{fig:e-cal}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Difference in Energy Scale}

The reconstructed \FD energy is different across both presented methods, as 
evident in \cref{fig:e-fd-drift}. The parameters of the drum energy scale 
(i.e.~\cref{eq:drum-energy-scale}) are in disagreement with the XY-Scanner 
energy scale (i.e.~\cref{eq:xy-energy-scale}). \cref{fig:calib-comparison} 
indicates that by using the proposed calibration tracking algorithm from 
\cref{sssec:constant-drift-model} the reconstructed energies in the \SD and \FD
increase by approximately \SIrange{10}{20}{\percent}.

\section{Summary}

In this chapter we have presented the XY-Scanner, a novel end-to-end 
calibration system set to become the standard absolute calibration technique 
for the \FD.

By utilizing a movable integrating sphere to illuminate the telescope aperture,
the XY-Scanner achieves an uncertainty on the pixel calibration constants 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ of \todo{uncertainty}, representing a significant improvement over the legacy drum calibration method.

{\vspace{0.5cm}\noindent\large\sc{Quality assurance for the XY-Scanner}\vspace{0.2cm}} 

We established a comprehensive quality assurance framework to validate the 
measurement results of the XY-Scanner data. The proposed quality cuts reject 
\SI{12}{\percent} of initial measurement runs due to factors such as 
temperature instability, positional misalignment, and aperture occlusion. A
comparison between rejected and non-rejected runs reveals that outliers in 
$C^i_\mathrm{abs}$ are effectively removed, and the standard deviation 
$\upsigma$ of the accepted dataset decreases more than fourfold.

{\vspace{0.5cm}\noindent\large\sc{Evidence of Telescope Ageing}\vspace{0.2cm}} 

The first XY-Scanner production dataset, comprising 11,880 calibration 
constants across all 27 FD telescopes, reveals a substantial discrepancy with 
the standard calibration constants that have been measured by the drum. The 
XY-Scanner constants are systematically higher by up to \SI{40}{\percent}.

Investigation into the causes of this aging identifies dust accumulation on 
mirrors and filters as one possible mechanism. Analysis of mirror cleaning 
campaigns demonstrate that cleaning restores light collection efficiency by 
\SIrange{8}{15}{\percent}. Furthermore, an analysis of 15 years of \NSB 
variance data confirms a long-term, non-recurrent drift in telescope 
sensitivity of approximately \SI{-1.97}{\percent} per year, driven by the 
combined effects of mirror contamination and PMT ageing, with only the latter 
being tracked by the current calibration algorithm.

{\vspace{0.5cm}\noindent\large\sc{Novel \SD/\FD Energy Scale}\vspace{0.2cm}} 

To address these deficiencies, we propose an alternative calibration tracking 
algorithm, utilizing the \NSB as an end-to-end proxy for total telescope 
sensitivity. By applying a constant drift model to the XY-Scanner reference 
constants, the long-term instability in the reconstructed energy of hybrid 
events is effectively resolved. 

We note a systematic increase of \SIrange{10}{20}{\percent} in reconstructed 
shower energies from using this alternative tracking algorithm. While the 
presented study is only preliminary, and the proposed tracking model very 
rudimentary in nature, these findings help us understand the temporal evolution
of the \FD hardware better, and underscore the necessity of updating the 
relative calibration tracking algorithm to monitor the entire optical path 
through the telescopes. 
